    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** serving as our undisputed anchor. With an elite score of 82.38, he is the must-start of the day; despite a slight reduction for facing a pitcher with a competent pickoff move, his matchup against Grant Holmes (4.65 xERA) provides the perfect environment for his elite speed to dictate the 5x5 landscape. We are also leaning heavily on **Josh Naylor** (72.72) and **Seiya Suzuki** (68.78), both of whom benefit from favorable park factors and exploitable starting pitchers. **Mickey Moniak** (66.41) remains a high-upside play; while he faces the formidable Logan Webb, the aggressive platoon advantage (+14%) outweighs the risk of the matchup, making him a primary driver for our offensive output.\n\nOur strategy for maximizing SB efficiency is bolstered by **Brice Turang** (66.25) and **Austin Riley** (60.98). **Brice Turang** thrives in today\u2019s conditions, where a combination of wind out and a favorable matchup against Justin Wrobleski creates a clear path to production. Conversely, we are keeping **Tyler Soderstrom** on the bench with a score of 54.71. He was the closest call for the optimizer, but his negative platoon adjustment against the left-handed MacKenzie Gore ultimately relegated him to the bench in favor of more efficient platoon-aligned starters. We continue to monitor the status of **James Wood** and **Giancarlo Stanton**, who remain sidelined on the IL, as well as the daily lineup confirmations for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Cole Young**, **Max Muncy**, **Zach McKinstry**, and **Jose Caballero**, whose projected production is contingent on their placement in the batting order. All managers should remain alert for late scratches, as our depth\u2014including **Trevor Story**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte**\u2014must be ready to pivot if official orders shift drastically.",
